WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:10.280
I've always been really passionate

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.660
about the challenge of thinking forward

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.023
on what our things are made
out of that we use every day.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.120
I do like to create something
that speaks for itself,

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.320
so somebody can look at it

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.290
and see the craftsmanship
that goes into it,

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:27.090
and also make them think about
how it was created and why,

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.070
what the motivation is?

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.940
And that keeps me inspired to
really push the ball forward

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.983
in terms of how a surfboard is made.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.520
We're surfers, we're in the ocean,

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.770
and we have a reverence for
the place that we're in.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:44.700
I think it's kind of paying
homage to that as well.

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:11.620
Surfboard sales, particularly
in the last few years

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.850
have absolutely gone through the roof.

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:16.990
And that's both a good
thing and a bad thing.

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.210
It means that there's probably three times

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:20.850
as many surfers in the water now,

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:23.720
which means three times as many surfboards

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.780
that are made from
unsustainable materials,

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:30.230
like fossil fuels, oils, resins,

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:33.083
things that are actually
used to make most surfboards.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:39.830
And so, people have been
exploring original material

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:41.920
for building surfboards like wood

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.620
in really innovative and unique ways.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.910
Danny Hess here in San Francisco

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:48.810
is probably the leading light on that.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.883
My goal is to make them
accessible for people.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.350
We're not trying to
mass-produce surfboards here.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.420
We're not trying to make as many as we can

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:06.940
as fast as we can.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:09.040
I'm just focused on creating boards

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:12.893
that the craftsmanship
and quality is foremost.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.130
One of the greatest motivations was

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:19.640
that I was taking salvaged wood,

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:21.280
and really just trying
to push the ball forward

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.950
and just see what alternative materials

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:25.100
that aren't surfboard materials

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.800
could make a successful surfboard.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.820
Foam is just a dead piece of material.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:33.023
It doesn't have a spring or a life to it.

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:39.010
Whereas wood is, you know,
you bend a piece of wood,

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:40.410
it springs right back to it.

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:44.470
Surfboards right now are not sustainable

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:46.710
except for wooden surfboards.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:49.330
And we need to move millions
and millions of people

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:53.280
towards products like those
that are built to last,

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.387
built from dramatically
sustainable materials.

00:02:55.387 --> 00:02:58.060
Materials that actually sequester carbon,

00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:59.770
you know, out of the air and lock it away

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:01.770
in the surfboard under your feet.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.500
Just thinking about the materials

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:07.440
that go into the surfboards
that we use is really important

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.220
because I don't want to
be surfing a surfboard

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.913
that has to be replaced every year.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.800
The wood kind of is the starting point

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.610
and dictates what kind of
board we're gonna build.

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:22.640
I use a lot of different
locally sourced salvaged wood

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.680
from walnut to redwood, cypress,

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:28.810
all kinds of different wood.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:30.450
The wood that I'm
building this board out of

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:31.580
is pretty special wood.

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:34.820
It's out of an old Victorian
from San Francisco.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:38.890
So I select the pieces that
are gonna be appropriate,

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:40.673
bring that into the shop here.

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:44.153
And I use the chop saw to cut to length.

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:52.693
The panels start out pretty
raw. The edges are rough.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.050
There's not a true
straight edge on anywhere.

00:03:56.990 --> 00:03:59.510
And so I use the Festool track saw

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:01.840
that goes into the track,

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.570
and cuts incredibly accurate straight line

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:06.103
through the panel of wood.

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:14.930
And then I'm taking
those two separate pieces

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:17.690
and gluing them together
to create the panel

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.523
that will become the surfboard.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.170
Once I create the panels,

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:27.640
I template the outline of the board

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:30.350
into the top of those panels.

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:32.640
So you end up with essentially the outline

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:34.883
of the surfboard in a
flat panel of redwood.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:40.180
After that, I take that to the band saw

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:43.723
and rough cut the
outline of the surfboard.

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:47.560
What I'm doing is creating a top panel,

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:50.763
a bottom panel that are
the shape of the surfboard.

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:56.100
I also rough cut the frame
parts that create the interior

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.760
and rail parts of the surfboard.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:01.460
And those are the banana-shaped pieces

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:04.150
that you see me cutting out.

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:05.350
Essentially, what they're doing

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:09.290
is creating a hollow cavity
inside the surfboard,

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:10.940
which removes a ton of weight,

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:13.490
but also makes them really strong.

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:15.783
So I create those frame parts.

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:19.990
And I take them onto the
table router and true them up

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:22.313
so that each part is
identical to each other.

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:25.560
And then I take it into the shaping room

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:29.853
and use a Japanese hand plane
as well as a belt sander.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.360
That creates a nice true part

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.190
that has nice clean edges on it.

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:37.760
And I do that on all the parts

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:39.410
'cause you want to have clean pieces

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:41.983
that you can accurately cut and assemble.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:46.020
I've made thousands of surfboards.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:50.863
So I really feel like I've
developed a real trust in my eye.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:57.100
It took me many, many
surfboards to get to that point

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.800
where I feel very confident
and comfortable with that.

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:04.470
Surfboards are essentially
a blend of art and science.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.600
And you really have to do the
science part, the measuring,

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:08.140
the math, and everything at the beginning.

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:10.700
And then when you are
really into the curves,

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:12.530
and the complex radiuses of the board,

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:15.150
you really have to trust the art of it.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:17.540
About 2,000 boards into it,

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:19.310
I started feeling like I just know

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:20.760
what this needs to look like.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.790
But it took a lot of measuring

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:28.323
and concentrating to get to that point.

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:37.270
So the rocker is super fundamental part

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:39.710
of what makes the surfboard work.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:42.620
It's the bottom curve of the surfboard.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:43.453
If you have a flat surfboard,

00:06:43.453 --> 00:06:46.540
it's just gonna go really fast,
but the nose is gonna poke,

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:47.550
and you're gonna go over the front.

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:49.383
If you have a really rockered surfboard,

00:06:49.383 --> 00:06:51.240
it's just gonna push water and bog.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.820
So you need to have a accurate curve.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:56.860
So to create that curved frame,

00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:59.550
I take those two interior frame parts,

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:01.950
and I roll glue between them.

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:05.440
I use this 10 minute
cure time really fast,

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:06.700
expanding polyurethane glue.

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:09.430
It's really amazing stuff,
but it kicks really fast.

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:11.850
So when you roll it out,
all your clamps are ready,

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:13.860
your parts are ready, you're ready.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:16.520
And you throw it in the
mold, clamp it down,

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.920
and just run those clamps
around it really fast.

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:24.093
It sits in that mold for two
hours under all that pressure.

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:30.023
Then you pull all the clamps
off and it holds that curve.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:33.640
A lot of wooden surfboards back in the day

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:35.900
were made from big blocks of wood,

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:37.740
and they would carve all that wood out.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:40.390
So there's all this wasted
wood in the nose and tail.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.680
Whereas with this, I'm
maximizing the use of the wood

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:47.290
that I'm using and
bending it into the curve,

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:51.600
which I think creates a much
stronger surfboard, the frame.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:53.080
And also just is way more efficient,

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:55.290
you're not wasting any of the wood.

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:57.290
And all wooden surfboard is gonna beat

00:07:57.290 --> 00:08:00.680
a surfboard that's made
from petroleum materials

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:02.280
any day, all day.

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:04.780
It's gonna outlast it.
It's gonna outperform it.

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:06.810
So I wanna make sure to give Danny

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:08.650
the props that he deserves.

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:11.060
His boards will be around
for hundreds of years

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:14.050
because they are tough,
because they don't break,

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:15.650
and they have great performance.

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:18.450
People are gonna want to keep
them and keep them around.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:23.540
For the next step,

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:25.670
I take it into the shaping room.

00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:26.720
The shaping room was designed

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:28.523
with really specific side lights.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:33.293
I take it in there,

00:08:33.293 --> 00:08:36.673
and strip all the glue off to
just get it all cleaned up.

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:41.410
I fine-tune it with my power planer

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:42.743
to clean up all the edges.

00:08:44.170 --> 00:08:46.030
And then I take the power planer

00:08:46.030 --> 00:08:49.190
and I put the foil into the frame.

00:08:49.190 --> 00:08:50.180
The foil is,

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:52.880
if you think about the way
an airplane wing looks,

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:55.530
if you look at the profile
of it, it's elliptical.

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:57.150
Surfboards have a foil to them

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:58.930
just as an airplane wing does.

00:08:58.930 --> 00:09:02.020
So you're putting that
foil into the bottom

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:03.633
and the top of that frame.

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:08.070
And that gives you a
refined internal panel piece

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:10.890
that you take back into the rocker bed,

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:15.250
and then take the deck panel,
which is a full panel of wood,

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:19.130
and bend that back into
the rocker bed with glue,

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:21.633
and clamp it all up all the way down.

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:24.850
Each piece is different.

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:26.370
When you try and do 'em
all at the same time,

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:27.690
they can crack and break,

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:29.550
and they become really temperamental.

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:31.340
You're just asking too
much out of the wood.

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:33.170
When you do it in steps like that,

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:35.660
you're able to really manipulate the wood

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:38.463
in a way that you wouldn't, if
you really tried to force it.

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:46.860
At this point, I've
glued together the deck,

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:49.683
the interior frame, and
the bottom of the board.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:54.330
And it's essentially
looking like a surfboard.

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:56.453
It has the rough outline of a surfboard.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:09:58.850
And that's when you start

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:01.180
laying your master template on there.

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:03.400
And that creates your guide points

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:06.460
for really drawing the curve.

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:08.830
Every shaper has their master templates

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:10.180
and the templates that they've created

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:13.390
over just many years of
evolving their designs.

00:10:13.390 --> 00:10:16.680
And this one is a
template I call The Bella.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:19.200
It's named after a friend's daughter.

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:22.080
The marks that I'm marking
on the deck of the surfboard

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:25.600
basically dictate the width
that the surfboard needs to be.

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:28.030
And then you're marking the
nose and tail dimensions.

00:10:28.030 --> 00:10:29.710
Those dimensions are all super important

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:32.850
to create a really nice blending curve

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:34.563
that is pleasing to your eye.

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:43.330
At this point, I really sharpen my tools.

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:45.070
Especially on old salvaged redwood,

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:47.470
you need to have really tuned sharp tools.

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:50.610
And so I'll take out my sharpening blocks,

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:53.783
and really put some time
into getting razor sharp.

00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:59.580
At that point, you have your shape set.

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:01.347
You have your length set,
you have your width set,

00:11:01.347 --> 00:11:03.497
and you have all your
other dimensions set.

00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:06.990
So from there, I take up the power planer

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:09.270
and really start truing up the outline

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:11.220
to the shape that the board's gonna be.

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:15.210
And once I true up the
outline of the board,

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:17.870
I flip it over and sand out the bottom

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:19.780
with my Festool sander.

00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:21.540
And that really evens the whole bottom out

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:23.130
and blends it all.

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:27.060
From there, I map out my
rail bands on the bottom,

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:30.943
which are the curve of
the rail of the surfboard.

00:11:32.091 --> 00:11:33.440
And I take the power planer again,

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:35.820
and just start putting that rail band in.

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:39.020
So it's from sharp edge to soft edge,

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:41.620
to sharper edge from nose to tail.

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:46.360
And you bring that in until
you feel the right curvature.

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:48.430
Then I take the Japanese block plane,

00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:50.010
and I really tune that rail.

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:52.023
I remove any little ridge.

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:54.640
And then I lift it up.

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:57.480
And the underarm test is
the true test of shaper.

00:11:57.480 --> 00:11:58.900
Like, you put it under your arm

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:00.380
and you feel that bottom edge.

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:03.410
And you know when it gets to
the point where it's right.

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:05.190
Then I take my sanding stick,

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:07.510
and I just shape in and blend in

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:09.900
all those little rough edges

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:12.600
that are left from the power
tools and the block plane.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:14.830
And then I flip it over onto the deck,

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:18.190
and start taking the
material out of the deck.

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:20.640
That process is removing
a lot of material.

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:23.580
And once I get that to
the point that I want it,

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:25.600
I take the Festool sander,

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:29.683
and really blend in all
those rough shaving marks.

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:33.900
Then I'm using the power planer

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:36.663
to blend the top rail
into the bottom rail.

00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:40.920
I'm putting that curve into
the board at that point.

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:44.380
And essentially, this is
the point where it's gone

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:48.333
from becoming a block
of wood to a surfboard.

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:54.163
And then I sand it,
fine-tuning those curves.

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:01.560
Then the shape of the board is done,

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:04.643
that it's finished, sanded,
it's ready for fin markings.

00:13:06.710 --> 00:13:09.960
This one, is specifically, is a twin fin.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:14.100
So I take my fin template,
mark the outline of the fin,

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:16.600
rough cut that out on the band saw,

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:20.597
and then true that up on
the stationary belt sander.

00:13:20.597 --> 00:13:23.030
And then take that outline of
the fin into my shaping room,

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:24.983
and clamp it down on my shaping stand.

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:29.770
And take my angle grinder
and put in the foil,

00:13:29.770 --> 00:13:32.330
which is that teardrop shape.

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:33.240
And then you have a fin

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:36.263
that's ready to be cut into
the bottom of that board.

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:41.350
And then I just put my
Hess stamp on the tail,

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:45.160
write the dimensions, which
are the length, the width,

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:47.290
the thickness of the board.

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:49.860
From there, I take my silk screen,

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:52.850
and stick it onto the deck of the board.

00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:55.500
Silk-screen it on there, flip over,

00:13:55.500 --> 00:13:57.223
do the same on the bottom.

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:01.540
And essentially, you've
got a finished board

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:02.990
that just needs to be sealed.

00:14:08.940 --> 00:14:11.780
I have this specific
natural oil called Monocoat

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:13.920
that I use to seal board.

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:15.063
And it's a really amazing oil

00:14:15.063 --> 00:14:17.640
that just penetrates
deep into the redwood,

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:21.483
and really creates a shell
or a skin that repels water.

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:24.410
I oil the fins at that point,

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:28.650
put my special glue on the very
base of the fin, the bottom,

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:31.090
and wedge it into the slot,

00:14:31.090 --> 00:14:33.860
make sure the fins in
the exact right position.

00:14:33.860 --> 00:14:35.370
And then essentially there you go,

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:36.927
you have a finished surfboard.

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:49.470
So at this point, the board's done,

00:14:49.470 --> 00:14:50.800
and you just have to be patient,

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:53.210
wait for the oil to dry, the fins to dry.

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:55.050
And then I usually get so excited.

00:14:55.050 --> 00:14:56.850
I just paddle out in
whatever waves I can find,

00:14:56.850 --> 00:14:59.380
and just 'cause I know
I wanna feel the board,

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:01.220
I wanna feel how it moves to the water,

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:02.110
and that's the best part.

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:05.510
As soon as you kind of
baptize the surfboard,

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:08.640
you feel how it pushes under waves.

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:11.610
And you know as soon as
you paddle for a wave,

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:15.150
it engages, it catches the
wave smoothly, quickly.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:17.190
And that first bottom
turn tells you so much

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:19.850
in terms of how that board's gonna work.

00:15:19.850 --> 00:15:22.270
'Cause each board has its own personality.

00:15:22.270 --> 00:15:23.930
That's such an exciting part

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:26.584
because there's so much anticipation.

00:15:26.584 --> 00:15:29.100
I have this idea, I wanna make
this surfboard work this way

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:30.190
for this specific wave.

00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:34.080
And then that moment of
truth, yes, it works!

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:35.280
That's the best feeling.

00:15:36.440 --> 00:15:37.920
Surfboard builders like Danny Hess

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:40.560
are not just a symbol of
sustainability for our sport,

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:42.540
they're the absolute pinnacle, right?

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:45.660
Our hope is that the rest
of the surfboard industry

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:48.240
can adopt some of those same practices

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:50.430
that Danny and others have,

00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:52.070
particularly with wooden surfboards,

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:55.810
and incorporate them into
the mass-producer for it.

00:15:55.810 --> 00:15:58.170
So that we're dramatically
lowering the impact

00:15:58.170 --> 00:15:59.240
that they're making,

00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:02.783
and hopefully even leaving a
positive legacy in their wake.

